<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Mandubii</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 52 BCE (approx.) · capital Alesia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Gallic people of Burgundy whose hilltop town Alesia was the scene of Vercingetorix's last stand against Caesar in 52 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mandubii lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxois">Auxois</a> district of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lingones">Lingones</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a>, and were probably clients of the Aedui. Their oppidum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia">Alesia</a> stood on Mont Auxois, above modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is known of their society before the siege that made their town famous.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 52 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia">Alesia</a>, and Caesar surrounded it with double lines of siege works. To save food, the defenders expelled the Mandubii with their wives and children, but Caesar would not let them pass, and they were left to starve between the lines. After the surrender Alesia lived on as a Gallo-Roman town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 300 BCE – 52 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:53.73%;width:4.94%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 52 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alesia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=mandubii" aria-label="Mandubii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4785 511 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mandubii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5105 767L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5105 767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5105 767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=mandubii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

